  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Why the letters are round",
      "body": "<p>Burmese, like Odia and Sinhala, was written for centuries on palm leaves with a stylus. Straight lines along the leaf would split it, so the letters came to be built from circles, half circles and small hooks. The script itself came from the Mon, who had taken it from southern India; it reached the Burmans in the 11th century, and Shan, Karen and Mon are written with versions of it today.</p>\n<p>The circles make some letters look alike. Watch where a circle opens and what is added to it: <span lang=\"my\">ဝ</span> is a closed circle, <span lang=\"my\">ဂ</span> opens at the top, and <span lang=\"my\">င</span> opens at the bottom.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Tones in the spelling",
      "body": "<p>Burmese has three tones, and a fourth kind of syllable that ends in a catch in the throat:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"my\">ကာ</td><td>low: long and level</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"my\">ကား</td><td>high: long, often a little breathy; written with း</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"my\">က့</td><td>creaky: short and tight; written with ့ or no mark</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"my\">ကက်</td><td>checked: cut off by a glottal stop</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The vowel sign shows the default tone, and the marks change it. The sounds in this app are spoken without tones, so listen to a native speaker for the melody.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Medials: letters that cling",
      "body": "<p>Burmese can put y, r, w or h between a consonant and its vowel by writing a medial sign on the consonant instead of a second letter: <span lang=\"my\">ျ</span> on the right, <span lang=\"my\">ြ</span> wrapped around, <span lang=\"my\">ွ</span> below, <span lang=\"my\">ှ</span> as a tail below.</p>\n<p>In modern speech ky and kr are both said ch: <span lang=\"my\">ကျောင်း</span> (school) is spelled kyaung and said chaung, and <span lang=\"my\">မြန်မာ</span> (Myanmar) is said myanma, with the r turned into a y.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "The asat and final sounds",
      "body": "<p>A consonant at the end of a syllable carries the asat, <span lang=\"my\">်</span>, a small curl on top that kills its vowel. Final stops are not said fully: k, t and p all become a catch in the throat, and n, m and ng all become a nasal vowel.</p>\n<p>So <span lang=\"my\">ကြက်</span> (chicken) is spelled kr-a-k but said chet, with a catch at the end.</p>"
    }
  ]
}
